WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.990
- [Narrator] Big Ben
(energetic electronic music)

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.640
is tilting to the north

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.880
and keeps leaning about
a millimeter a year.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.330
The 58 story Millennium Tower
in San Francisco has sunk

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.680
over a foot since it opened in
2009 according to a lawsuit.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.920
And the former construction
manager for this 57 story,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.470
77 million building in Manhattan

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.020
said it's leaning about three inches.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.780
Its developer says it's
structurally stable.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.480
Still, the building sits unfinished.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.141
The Millennium Tower has
undergone a 100 million

00:00:30.141 --> 00:00:33.750
construction project to
fix the unanticipated

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.753
movement according to its owner.

00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:40.170
These issues all have one
main cause, the ground.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.410
So to build taller
buildings on imperfect soil,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:47.760
developers are using an old
technology for a new purpose.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.860
It's called deep soil mixing.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.660
- I think it has been a game changer,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.160
completely game changer.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.657
- [Narrator] But there are risks.

00:00:54.657 --> 00:00:56.254
Here's how it works.

00:00:56.254 --> 00:00:57.210
(energetic electronic music)

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.095
The incredible weight of skyscrapers

00:00:59.095 --> 00:01:02.370
requires a strong foundation to stand on.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.110
In Miami, Andres Baquerizo

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.620
is helping to build the city's
first super tall skyscraper,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.300
the Waldorf Astoria Hotel

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.720
and Residences Miami on
ground that looks like this.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.660
Layers of porous limestone and sand.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.600
Not ideal for a 100 story tower.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.776
So Baquerizo and the team are literally

00:01:20.776 --> 00:01:24.630
changing the earth using deep soil mixing.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:25.463
- At the end,

00:01:25.463 --> 00:01:27.420
what you end up doing is
improving the capacity

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.130
of the soil to withstand better loads

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.130
and support buildings
or different conditions.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.540
- [Narrator] To do this, workers bring

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.260
in a high torque or
really powerful drill rig.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.802
This big blade carves
out wells in the ground

00:01:39.802 --> 00:01:42.750
that can be up to 110 feet deep.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.120
Here, they'll be around 40.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.100
- [Andres] Imagine your kitchen
blender, but in this case,

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.080
it's a nine to 10 foot diameter blender.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.280
This blender has some blades that

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.570
are going around the ground

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.510
and is shooting cement
through those blades.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.490
- [Narrator] Then cement
mixes with the soil

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:59.730
to form a super strong
slurry called soilcrete,

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.610
giving the soil a higher load capacity.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.660
- So what you end up taking

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.190
is a soil that would only have

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.510
a certain amount of capacity,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.520
and you'll increase the
compression capacity,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.762
the lateral capacity on it

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.580
and you're gonna make it really stiff,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.950
making it stiff and almost impermeable.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:14.994
- [Instructor] These wells are arranged

00:02:14.994 --> 00:02:16.680
in an overlapping grid,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.710
so that all of the soil is properly mixed.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.480
- Every one of these cells, right?

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.261
That little circle there that seems little

00:02:23.261 --> 00:02:25.800
is really between nine
to 10 foot diameter.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:26.804
- [Narrator] It also makes the ground

00:02:26.804 --> 00:02:28.980
almost impervious to water,

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.070
which at waterside properties
like the Waldorf Astoria,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.290
means you don't have to fight the tide.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.690
- Before, when you were
having the water being run,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.570
it's a race against the water
to when you can do the work

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.820
if you're gonna have
a high tide, low tide.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:42.810
- [Narrator] Deep soil mixing

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.260
can also help buildings
withstand natural disasters

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.630
like earthquakes and the hurricanes

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.840
seen in Miami when wind can cause problems

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.520
for super tall buildings.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.770
- [Narrator] What happens
typically with winds

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.140
is that it will push the
building to try to tilt

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:59.070
and when it's tilting,

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.350
it's literally push the building sideways.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.330
But more importantly, in the foundation,

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.010
if you realize if it's going this way,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.380
one side is pushing and
the other one is lifting.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.540
So now you have a tension condition

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.070
and a compression condition.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.029
If you increase the capacity

00:03:12.029 --> 00:03:14.302
of the piles to not be bent laterally

00:03:14.302 --> 00:03:16.127
and increase the compression capacity,

00:03:16.127 --> 00:03:17.760
and a little bit of the tension,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.850
it's gonna end up helping the
building to be more stable.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:21.683
- [Narrator] Deep soil mixing's

00:03:21.683 --> 00:03:24.600
ground-hardening abilities
not only make a building

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.750
like this possible, they
make it more profitable.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.610
For example, developers have been able

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.650
to add more high floor condos

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.870
than they would have if
the building was shorter.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.910
They'll start at $10 million each

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.340
and will look like these renderings.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.260
- More people are willing
to say, you know what?

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.700
It's interesting, let's go for it.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:42.819
So you're beginning to see this increase

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:44.036
of the size of the city

00:03:44.036 --> 00:03:46.230
and the height of the buildings.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.700
- [Narrator] The
construction company building

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.150
the Waldorf Astoria Tower is
now using deep soil mixing

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.070
for shorter residential projects

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.667
across the country and around the world.

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:57.990
They've used it here, here,

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.929
and here to prevent soil
shifting under skyscrapers,

00:04:01.929 --> 00:04:05.040
and that's just one company's projects.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.126
Other companies also
have projects underway

00:04:07.126 --> 00:04:09.150
across the globe,

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.790
but the method comes with some risks.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.530
- So if a building was
demolished to be able

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:16.740
to put a new building and they
left anything made by man,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.480
the big blender is coming into the ground

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.670
and shooting cement will
hit this obstruction

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.010
and may shift out of
location or damage the tool.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.150
If you get shift out of location

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.670
and you don't realize that it happened,

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:27.810
then during the excavation process,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.820
you wind up getting some water intrusion.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.310
- [Narrator] That's when water
flows into the excavation.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.040
A small amount is normal,
but any flow larger

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.770
than a garden hose can be hard to fix.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.258
Take a nearby Miami
project, UNA Residences,

00:04:40.258 --> 00:04:41.910
as an example.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:45.570
In March, 2022, water entered
the unfinished foundation

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:46.920
during deep soil mixing,

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.591
creating what looked like a lagoon

00:04:48.591 --> 00:04:51.210
and concerning residents.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.230
The developers said that
construction is back underway

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.500
and some land like beach
sand is just too soft

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.000
for deep soil mixing.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.860
If the technique is used on soil

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.330
that's not quite right,

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.090
the layer of soilcrete can crack.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.320
- When it begins to break,

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.270
the difficult thing is, in most cases,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:10.770
you almost have to start from scratch,

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.870
because if you already
found you have a weakness,

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.332
it's not something you can reinforce

00:05:14.332 --> 00:05:18.060
as the water will always want to come in.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.490
- [Narrator] Previously, deep
soil mixing was mainly used

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.100
for industrial sites like oil refineries

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.280
where the land needed
to hold heavy equipment.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.633
- Miami was the first
city, where at some point,

00:05:28.633 --> 00:05:31.477
the industry realized that this condition

00:05:31.477 --> 00:05:34.290
could help us improve the
capacity of onsite soils.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.749
And from Miami, there has been developing

00:05:35.749 --> 00:05:38.970
is now beginning to go into
other places of the world.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.935
- [Narrator] But due to the
unique engineering challenges

00:05:40.935 --> 00:05:43.890
that Miami's sandy soil presents,

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.360
Andres's team of engineers

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.090
are paying close attention
to the construction.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.400
The progress the team makes here will help

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.280
inform the company's
engineers around the globe

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.270
on how to make more stable
ground for their buildings.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.350
- As we use it more,

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.407
we have been able to
understand better the system

00:06:00.407 --> 00:06:02.883
to keep using their benefits

00:06:02.883 --> 00:06:04.343
into the benefit of the buildings.

00:06:04.343 --> 00:06:06.453
So for me, has been a game changer.

